A||||1||theogre||Filters, Tranny. A note for those changing fluid.
Z||000000||theogre||07-25-99||01:12 PM||||On the subject of filters,,,,, the tranny filter for the TH125 has been modified a time or 2 over the years. If you have a pre 86-7 car, you may need to fool with the filter clip to get the filter clear of the pan. If you can find the newer clip, it eliminates the problem. Your local trany shop, parts dealer, might have the new, but bending the old clip to clear is fine.<p>
Z||000001||theogre||07-26-99||08:54 AM||||Want to get as much oil out of the tranny as you can, and reduce the mess when the pan comes off to change the filter?<p>Disconnect the bottom cooling line from the tranny and connect a scrap line to the tranny. Then start the car and let it IDLE untill the oil stops flowing. Shut off the car, let it sit 5 min then idle it again for a minute or 2. This scond pass lets some of the oil drain back from the TC.<p>DO NOT rev the car, or attempt to put it in gear!!!!!!!!!!! If you are worried about hurting the tranny you can pull the oil out the cooling port with a drill driven pump running at low speed. The tranny will hold enough oil that running the car to pump it down won't hurt it as long as you stop the motor when the oil stops flowing.<p>While you have the lines off, blow out the lines and heat exchanger. Replace any soft line that is worn.<p>I was able to get over 5 quarts of oil out of the tranny durring a filter change by pumping it down first. This still leaves a bit in the TC, but not as much as just dropping the pan only. I had a heck of allot less mess when I got done. The tranny shifts allot better now with 5.5 quarts of new fluid in it.<p>
